course: dictionary
lesson: 6909
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Oxford can be a place, a shoe, or a comma. The words around it tell you which one. «The Thames flows through Oxford, Reading and Windsor.» Rivers flow through places, so Oxford must be a city.
explain: «He wore a pair of black oxfords to the wedding.» You wear shoes on your feet, and this is a shoe. A small o means the shoe, but a capital O, as in Oxford, usually means a name.
explain: «Red, white, and blue» has a comma before «and». Some writers leave that comma out and some keep it. The one that keeps it is said to use the Oxford comma, so the word can name a style as well as a place.
fact: Oxford is a city in England.
fact: The University of Oxford is one of the oldest universities in the world.
fact: An oxford is a lace-up leather shoe.
fact: An Oxford comma is a comma before «and» in a list, as in «red, white, and blue».
fact: A capital letter, as in Oxford, often means a name, while a small letter, as in oxford, means an ordinary word.
exercise: The Thames flows through «Oxford, Reading and Windsor». What is Oxford here? | A comma in a list | A river near the sea | A lace-up leather shoe | *A city in England, famous for its very old university
exercise: «He wore a pair of black oxfords to the wedding.» What is an oxford here? | A comma before «and» | A kind of old university | A city in England | *A lace-up leather shoe
exercise: In the list «red, white, and blue», which comma is the Oxford comma? | The comma after «red» | *The comma before «and» | The comma at the end of the sentence | There is no comma in this list
test: «Yasmin says the oldest university is in Fez, Morocco; Tom says Oxford.» What do Yasmin and Tom disagree about? | The name of a shoe | Which river flows through Oxford | *Which university is the oldest | Whether a comma is needed
test: A spoonerism is named after William Spooner, «an Oxford teacher who was said to make such slips.» What does the word Oxford tell you about Spooner? | He wrote a book about commas | He made lace-up shoes | *He was a teacher in Oxford | He lived in Fez, Morocco
exam: On a rowing-boat trip, the story was first told to three sisters «on the river near Oxford», so where did this happen? | In a London shoe shop | At a school in Glasgow | In Fez, Morocco | *On the river near Oxford
